A branch is a movable reference to a commit object. A working tree
is a directory of files checked out from the index. Switching branches
in a dirty tree requires overwriting files on disk; Git refuses in order
to preserve your edits.

Stashing writes uncommitted changes into dangling commit objects. It is
messy, easy to lose, and strips your work of its filesystem context.

The clean solution is a second working tree. Each worktree maintains its
own index and HEAD pointer, but shares the underlying object store and refs.
Leave the dirty tree untouched and do the urgent work in a separate directory.
